Nope.

Take a closer look at the following code (in eat.c):

((monstermoves - otmp->age) > (int) otmp->blessed ? 50:30) &&

This expression does not, as it is apparent that its writer believed, 
compare the age of the object to either 50 or 30.

What it in fact does is test whether the age is greater than the value of 
blessed.  The whole expression then has value either 30 or 50, i.e., true.

Upshot being even freshly-spilled-out blessed-horn-of-plenty food will be
rotten 1/7th of the time ... and you can forget about keeping food in an 
icebox, it makes absolutely no difference whatsoever.

I would like to think that this is a bug.  Too bad it will never be fixed.

Has no-one *ever* noticed this?  Really?

